### 1. Where the WARN is emitted
`fs/kernfs/dir.c` — `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns()`:
```
int kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(struct kernfs_node *parent, const char *name, ...)
	if (!parent) {
		WARN(1, KERN_WARNING "kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\n", name);
		return -ENOENT;
```
Caller is `fs/sysfs/symlink.c`:
```
void sysfs_remove_link(struct kobject *kobj, const char *name)
	if (!kobj) ... else parent = kobj->sd;
	kernfs_remove_by_name(parent, name);
```
So `parent == NULL` means **the target kobject's sysfs directory (`kobj->sd`) is already gone** (or the named link never existed) at the moment someone tries to delete the link `"typec"`. `kobj->sd` is NULLed in `fs/sysfs/dir.c: sysfs_remove_dir()` (`kobj->sd = NULL;`), i.e. after `device_del()` of that device.

### 2. Who removes a link named exactly `"typec"`
Only one place in the whole Type-C class — `drivers/usb/typec/class.c`:
```
static void typec_partner_link_device(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)
	ret = sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, "typec");
	ret = sysfs_create_link(&partner->dev.kobj, &dev->kobj, dev_name(dev));
	    sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");        /* error unwind */
	if (partner->attach) partner->attach(partner, dev);

static void typec_partner_unlink_device(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)
	sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev));
	sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");            <-- the WARN comes from here
	if (partner->deattach) partner->deattach(partner, dev);
```
`dev` here is a **USB device** (`struct usb_device`), tracked in the port as `usb2_dev` / `usb3_dev` (`drivers/usb/typec/class.h`: `struct device *usb2_dev; ... *usb3_dev;`, plus `struct mutex partner_link_lock;`).

### 3. The exact offending chain

`drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c`:
```
static void ucsi_handle_connector_change(struct work_struct *work)   /* kworker/6:0, events wq */
	... if (change & UCSI_CONSTAT_CONNECT_CHANGE) ... ucsi_unregister_partner(con);

static void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)
	if (!con->partner) return;
	typec_set_mode(con->port, TYPEC_STATE_SAFE);
	typec_partner_set_usb_power_delivery(con->partner, NULL);
	ucsi_unregister_partner_pdos(con);
	ucsi_unregister_altmodes(con, UCSI_RECIPIENT_SOP);
	ucsi_unregister_cable(con);
	typec_unregister_partner(con->partner);
	con->partner = NULL;
```
`drivers/usb/typec/class.c`:
```
void typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)
	if (!partner) return;
	port = to_typec_port(partner->dev.parent);
	mutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);
	if (port->usb2_dev) { typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb2_dev); port->usb2_dev = NULL; }
	if (port->usb3_dev) { typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb3_dev); port->usb3_dev = NULL; }
	mutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);
	device_unregister(&partner->dev);
```

So: `ucsi_handle_connector_change` → `ucsi_unregister_partner` → `typec_unregister_partner` → `typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb2_dev|usb3_dev)` → `sysfs_remove_link(&usbdev->kobj, "typec")` → `sysfs_remove_link()` sees `kobj->sd == NULL` → `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(NULL, "typec")` → `WARN` + `-ENOENT`. Exactly the stack in the report.

### 4. Why `port->usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` is stale / unlinked

Two independent bookkeeping holes, both visible in this tree:

a) **No record that the link was ever created.** `typec_partner_link_device()` creates the link only when a partner exists; the pointers are set unconditionally in `typec_partner_attach()`:
```
static void typec_partner_attach(struct typec_connector *con, struct device *dev)
	struct typec_port *port = container_of(con, struct typec_port, con);
	if (udev->speed < USB_SPEED_SUPER) port->usb2_dev = dev; else port->usb3_dev = dev;
	partner = typec_get_partner(port);
	if (partner) { typec_partner_link_device(partner, dev); put_device(&partner->dev); }
```
If `sysfs_create_link()` failed, or the USB device attached before/around partner registration, `usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` is non-NULL while no `"typec"` link exists → the later unconditional `sysfs_remove_link()` warns.

b) **The stale-pointer path.** The pointers are only cleared in `typec_partner_deattach()`:
```
static void typec_partner_deattach(struct typec_connector *con, struct device *dev)
	partner = typec_get_partner(port); if (partner) typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, dev);
	if (port->usb2_dev == dev) port->usb2_dev = NULL;
	else if (port->usb3_dev == dev) port->usb3_dev = NULL;
```
which is reached only from `drivers/usb/core/hub.c: usb_disconnect()`:
```
	usb_disable_device(udev, 0);
	... typec_deattach(port_dev->connector, &udev->dev);
	usb_remove_ep_devs(&udev->ep0);
	device_del(&udev->dev);
```
i.e. **only if `port_dev->connector` is still set**. But `drivers/usb/core/port.c: connector_unbind()` drops the link without ever calling `typec_deattach()`:
```
static void connector_unbind(struct device *dev, struct device *connector, void *data)
	sysfs_remove_link(&connector->kobj, dev_name(dev));
	sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "connector");
	port_dev->connector = NULL;
```
Once that has happened (component/aggregate teardown, `typec_unlink_ports()` in `drivers/usb/typec/port-mapper.c`), the USB device can be `device_del()`'d — `sysfs_remove_dir()` sets `kobj->sd = NULL` — while `port->usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` still points at it and no refcount is held (`typec_partner_attach()` does *not* `get_device(dev)`). The next connector-change event then unlinks a dead kobject → the WARN. That is the root cause of the boot-time occurrence on this machine: the UCSI hotplug storm at boot races the USB port/connector component binding, so links are removed for devices whose sysfs dir is already gone or whose link was never created.

The `partner_link_lock` (`drivers/usb/typec/class.h`, initialized in `typec_register_port()`) only serializes the attach/deattach vs. register/unregister paths; it does **not** validate that the link exists or that `dev` is still registered — which is precisely what is missing.

### 5. Is a fix present in this tree?

No. rtk shows the removal is unconditional and unguarded — there is no `device_is_registered()`/`dev->kobj.sd` check, no `sysfs_remove_link_nowarn`-style variant, no per-device "linked" flag, and no `get_device()` on `usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev`:
- `drivers/usb/typec/class.c` — the only `sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec")` call sites are inside `typec_partner_link_device()` (error unwind) and `typec_partner_unlink_device()`.
- The only `WARN` in the file is unrelated (`WARN_ON(!try_module_get(adev->dev.driver->owner))`).
- `drivers/usb/core/port.c: connector_unbind()` still lacks a `typec_deattach()`.

### 6. Upstream fix

I can only inspect this source tree with rtk (no git history/network access here), so I will not quote a commit hash I cannot verify. What I can state from the code: the affected logic is `typec_partner_link_device()` / `typec_partner_unlink_device()` / `typec_partner_attach()` / `typec_partner_deattach()` in `drivers/usb/typec/class.c` plus `connector_bind()`/`connector_unbind()` in `drivers/usb/core/port.c`, and the `partner_link_lock` hardening series (which introduced `partner_link_lock`, `usb2_dev`, `usb3_dev` handling) is the relevant upstream work — the ENOENT WARN case is a follow-up gap in that same series, not yet fixed here. To confirm/locate the fix, check upstream `git log --oneline v6.17.. -- drivers/usb/typec/class.c drivers/usb/core/port.c` and the linux-usb thread for "kernfs: can not remove 'typec'".

Minimal fix shape consistent with this tree:
1. In `typec_partner_unlink_device()`, skip `sysfs_remove_link()` unless the device is still registered (`dev->kobj.sd`)/the link was recorded.
2. Take a reference (`get_device`) on `usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` in `typec_partner_attach()` and drop it where they are cleared.
3. Call `typec_deattach()` for `port_dev->child` in `connector_unbind()` so the port never keeps a stale pointer.
